WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
ChatGPT now handles one in five searches that used to go straight to Google. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 217. After that comes a deep dive on how chat answers get built. Cool stuff on tools that help design cars. Then quick bits. People, news bites reports, are choosing artificial intelligence chatbots, meaning programs you talk to in plain language, over traditional search engines at a record pace. Because a full question and conversation now feels easier than lining up

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.560
the perfect keywords. A search engine is a site that hands you a list of links and leaves

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.820
you to go hunting. A chatbot tries to answer you the way a person would. The old way was

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.460
like scanning the index jammed in the back of a school textbook, hunting for a page that

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.340
might help. You had to guess the exact word the book used, or you skipped right past the

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.800
section you needed. The new way is closer to putting your hand up in class and asking

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.700
someone who already knows the chapter. That person hears your whole question, remembers

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.420
is what you asked a minute ago and can hand you a direct answer instead of a pile of possible

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:09.260
pages. Picture walking up to a giant wall of closed doors. Classic Search still drops

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.880
that whole wall in front of you and leaves you to try the handles one by one. The chat

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.520
bot tries to walk you into the one room that matches what you actually meant, instead of

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.900
making you tour the hallway first. Searching used to feel like a separate skill you had

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.580
to practice, the way you once learned a library catalog. Now it feels more like talking, which

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:33.700
is a much lower bar for a tired brain. For a student stuck on homework, a creator hunting

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:39.040
for ideas, or anyone curious about something at 10 at night, that shift is faster and

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.320
more natural than opening 10 tabs and skimming. Google used to win by showing the best links.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.280
Chat GPT wins by giving the best single answer, and if the trend holds, finding information

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:55.960
keeps sliding from lists toward conversation. Companies now have to compete on that direct

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:01.020
answer. Not just on who can rank a blue link the highest. You can feel the difference in

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:06.160
about a minute on your phone or laptop. Open the chat GPT website and type any question

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.040
you would normally search. Notice how a full answer lands instead of a page of blue links.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.840
Then run the same question in both places side by side and see which one gets you moving faster.

00:02:17.863 --> 00:02:22.823
Your phone keyboard already guesses the next word while you text by looking at the last few words

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:25.823
and picking what usually follows from millions of other messages.

00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:30.583
That little strip of suggestions above the keys is a tiny cousin of the same trick.

00:02:31.103 --> 00:02:33.563
A chat model is that idea scaled way up.

00:02:34.003 --> 00:02:37.103
Instead of one word, it guesses the next little chunk of text.

00:02:37.483 --> 00:02:41.103
Then the next, again and again, until a full answer sits on the screen.

00:02:41.803 --> 00:02:44.063
Okay, this one's a little tricky, but stick with me.

00:02:44.543 --> 00:02:48.243
Those chunks are called tokens, which are basically Lego bricks of language.

00:02:48.723 --> 00:02:51.543
Sometimes a whole word, and sometimes just a piece of one.

00:02:51.543 --> 00:02:55.343
The process starts with your exact words as the first row of bricks.

00:02:55.943 --> 00:03:00.683
The model then scores thousands of possible next bricks, using patterns it learned from

00:03:00.683 --> 00:03:02.703
books, websites, and conversations.

00:03:03.443 --> 00:03:07.543
It snaps on the brick that fits best, then does the whole scoring step again.

00:03:08.143 --> 00:03:12.903
It is like adding beads to a necklace, one bead at a time, until the chain has grown into

00:03:12.903 --> 00:03:13.283
paragraphs.

00:03:14.023 --> 00:03:17.023
It is not fetching a saved page the way a search engine does.

00:03:17.523 --> 00:03:20.763
It is extending the pattern that seems to belong after your words.

00:03:20.763 --> 00:03:25.303
the same way you might finish someone's sentence in a group chat because you know how they talk.

00:03:25.903 --> 00:03:32.023
Because the training examples were huge, those probabilities often line up with what a helpful person would say next.

00:03:32.643 --> 00:03:35.263
That is why the replies can feel surprisingly natural.

00:03:35.863 --> 00:03:38.983
The core trick stays the same whether the model is writing a story,

00:03:39.483 --> 00:03:42.103
walking through a math problem, or helping with a recipe.

00:03:42.763 --> 00:03:46.123
Repeated next token prediction is one engine doing many jobs,

00:03:46.543 --> 00:03:48.703
with no separate program for each task.

00:03:48.703 --> 00:03:52.443
You do not need a computer science class to hold that picture in your head.

00:03:52.903 --> 00:03:55.443
A necklace growing bead by bead is the whole move,

00:03:55.843 --> 00:03:57.183
and now you can see it happening.

00:03:58.245 --> 00:04:02.405
Open AI in a post on X shared that chat GPT and Codex,

00:04:02.785 --> 00:04:05.865
their coding helper, are being used to create mechanical parts,

00:04:06.465 --> 00:04:08.845
electrical layouts, and even full vehicle designs.

00:04:09.645 --> 00:04:12.405
Electrical layouts means the path's electricity follows,

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:13.985
including circuit boards,

00:04:13.985 --> 00:04:18.645
those green boards inside gadgets that carry power from one tiny part to another.

00:04:19.265 --> 00:04:23.865
Engineers at Mazda and at a racing team are letting the AI turn three-dimensional scans

00:04:23.865 --> 00:04:25.325
into models they can edit.

00:04:25.905 --> 00:04:30.965
A three-dimensional scan is like a shape photocopy of a real object, capturing the curve

00:04:30.965 --> 00:04:32.565
so a computer can work with them.

00:04:33.065 --> 00:04:37.585
Then they run simulations, which means testing the design on a computer before anyone cuts

00:04:37.585 --> 00:04:37.945
metal.

00:04:38.465 --> 00:04:43.445
The AI suggests shapes and connections, and humans check the results for safety, and for

00:04:43.445 --> 00:04:45.765
whether the parts actually fit in the real world.

00:04:46.265 --> 00:04:51.125
Think of it as a super-fast intern who can sketch part after part overnight, while the

00:04:51.125 --> 00:04:54.345
experienced engineer still signs off before anything gets built.

00:04:54.925 --> 00:04:57.485
The intern is quick, the sign-off is still human.

00:04:58.045 --> 00:05:02.565
The same family of tools is stepping past essays and pictures into metal and plastic you

00:05:02.565 --> 00:05:03.385
can actually hold.

00:05:03.945 --> 00:05:08.545
One day, those workflows could help students design simple robots or custom parts for school

00:05:08.545 --> 00:05:09.045
projects.

00:05:09.045 --> 00:05:13.605
You can try a tiny version of that workflow right now with no special software.

00:05:14.125 --> 00:05:20.785
Open the Chat GPT website and describe a simple object you wish existed, like a phone stand that clips to a laptop.

00:05:21.605 --> 00:05:23.745
Ask it to list the parts and how they would connect.

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:28.105
Then ask it to suggest changes based on materials you actually have at home.

00:05:28.685 --> 00:05:31.565
And watch how the design shifts when cardboard is all you've got.

00:05:32.509 --> 00:05:36.489
A walkable pixel art world now sits on top of Chat GPT chats.

00:05:36.489 --> 00:05:39.549
Thanks to a free user script someone posted to Reddit.

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:44.389
A user script is a tiny add-on that changes how a website behaves in your browser.

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:49.929
And pixel art means those little blocky pictures, like an old video game you can wander through.

00:05:50.429 --> 00:05:55.289
The project is called elsewhere, and it runs right on the chat GPT website with no extra

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:56.069
developer key.

00:05:56.629 --> 00:06:00.669
Adventure Mode lets you solve stories by talking to characters and using objects.

00:06:01.229 --> 00:06:03.609
And Hangout Mode lets you build a personal space.

00:06:03.609 --> 00:06:09.309
Rearrange Furniture, redesign how things look, save worlds, and export files so you can

00:06:09.309 --> 00:06:13.369
continue later, chatting with chat GPT as a character inside the room.

00:06:13.869 --> 00:06:18.209
The script lives on greasy fork and needs a user script manager like violent monkey.

00:06:18.869 --> 00:06:23.689
World creation can take around 30 minutes and uses part of your normal chat GPT allowance.

00:06:24.289 --> 00:06:28.029
And after that, you can walk around and interact without regenerating everything.

00:06:28.029 --> 00:06:34.342
One user connected an OPPO A5 from 2020 to a computer over a USB cable.

00:06:34.722 --> 00:06:40.462
The normal cable you used to plug a phone into a laptop, and let GPT-6 Astro read the

00:06:40.462 --> 00:06:44.602
logs and carrier settings, without wiping the phone back to factory settings.

00:06:45.022 --> 00:06:50.322
The phone was on the 1NZ network, and calls went straight to voicemail until the AI spotted

00:06:50.322 --> 00:06:52.502
a single hidden setting that was blocking them.

00:06:52.982 --> 00:06:56.842
Like a light switch tucked behind the couch, and applied a temporary fix.

00:06:56.842 --> 00:07:02.142
The phone started working again, though the change resets on restart, which shows how

00:07:02.142 --> 00:07:06.522
this kind of AI can already dig into a technical problem when it is given the right access.

00:07:07.202 --> 00:07:08.102
That's it for today.

00:07:08.422 --> 00:07:12.062
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:07:12.542 --> 00:07:15.622
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:07:15.942 --> 00:07:17.442
That's literally how learning works.

00:07:18.002 --> 00:07:20.942
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

